60,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option C is the most rational choice. A bookable calendar removes the ambiguity of ""first-come, first-served"" and ensures fair access, while a professional purpose eliminates the desk crumbs and the need for external rentals.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Option C provides the structure Ryuji wants, but we must ensure it's used for more than just formal meetings. It should be a sanctuary for those difficult, private conversations we currently lack the space to have.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,May,,"I'm leaning toward C too, but only because Option A is a nightmare. A kitchen just means more scrubbing and arguing over whose turn it is to empty the bins. Let's go with the one that doesn't create more chores.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option C is the only logical conclusion. We've already proven we're wasting money on external rentals. Investing 141,000 yen now to stop a recurring leak is basic arithmetic. Let's stop debating ""feelings"" and act like professionals.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Servas,,"While Option C addresses the finances, we must be mindful of the walls. If we create a meeting space without addressing the sound, we only amplify the noise for those of us working beside it. Peace requires more than a calendar.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Servas, the calendar system in Option C provides a structured way to manage the room's use, which is the first step toward order. We can address noise through behavioral rules once the purpose is defined.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Ryuji, rules are a start, but the physical space must invite trust. If we choose Option C, let's agree it's a place for honest dialogue, not just agendas, so we can finally stop being strangers.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,May,,"@Jonathan, as long as ""honest dialogue"" doesn't involve someone spending an hour cleaning up after a ""heart-to-heart,"" I'm in. Option C is the only one that doesn't sound like a second job for whoever is the most helpful.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, your desire for a ""sanctuary"" is touching, but let's keep it grounded. Option C is a business decision. It solves the financial leak and the utility gap. The ROI is clear; the rest is just noise.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Shunichi, the ""noise"" you dismiss is the very thing that disrupts our focus. If we choose Option C, we must commit to a culture of quiet and respect, for a meeting room is only as useful as the peace it preserves.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Servas, a culture of respect is easier to maintain when there is a clear system in place. Option C replaces chaos with a schedule, ensuring that no one is unfairly excluded or burdened. It is the most sustainable path.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. If we use Option C to create a space where we can finally speak privately and honestly, we can bridge the distance between us. It is the best way to foster genuine connection.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, @Jonathan, you've sold me. As long as we're talking about a calendar and a screen rather than sponges and soap, I'm on board. Let's pick the option that doesn't eat up our free time.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, for once we agree. The cost of the fit-out is a drop in the bucket compared to the inefficiency of our current habits. Let's stop the bleeding and vote for Option C. It's the only adult choice here.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Servas,,"I see that we have found a common path. While my heart still worries for the silence of our workspace, I believe that a shared commitment to respect will outweigh the thinness of the walls. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"The consensus is clear. Option C provides the objective structure needed to eliminate unfairness and financial waste. By replacing ambiguity with a bookable system, we ensure a rational and sustainable use of the space. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I vote for Option C. Beyond the efficiency, I believe this space can finally give us the privacy we need to speak honestly and truly get to know one another. Let's use it to build a kinder workplace.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,May,,I vote for Option C. It's the only choice that doesn't turn into a part-time cleaning job for the rest of us. Let's keep the labor low and the efficiency high.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,I vote for Option C. It's the only decision that makes financial sense and treats this office like a business rather than a dormitory. Let's finally stop wasting money and get this sorted.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Servas,,"I vote for Option C. Let us move forward with a spirit of mindfulness, ensuring that our new space becomes a sanctuary of respect and quiet for all who dwell nearby.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
